  "network": {
    "net_event_lines": 0,
    "verdict": "No V6M0: NETWORK_EVENT line appeared, i.e. no network state transition was reported during the run.",
    "not_claimed": "This does not verify reconnect: no disconnect was provoked, so the reconnect path was never exercised. Absence of an event also cannot distinguish 'stable' from 'no transition logged'.",
    "open_observation": {
      "what": "The host issues a WiFi scan continuously: 138 'rpc_req: Scan start Req' and 138 'RPC_WRAP: ESP Event: StaScanDone' lines in 31.3 minutes, i.e. one scan roughly every 12.9s with about 2.9s of scan time (e.g. 867726 -> 870580 -> 883459 -> 896339 -> 909219, interval 12879ms).",
      "caller_evidence": [
        "'Scan start Req' is logged at managed_components/espressif__esp_hosted/host/drivers/rpc/core/rpc_req.c:441, so the scan request originates on the host (P4), not from the C5 autonomously.",
        "WifiStation::StartScan logs its own banner ('Scanning all channels' or 'Scanning saved channel N'); neither appears in any of the 30 segments, so this is not that entry point logging.",
        "BluFi provisioning is not compiled in: sdkconfig has only CONFIG_USE_HOTSPOT_WIFI_PROVISIONING=y.",
        "WifiConfigurationAp does own a periodic scan timer, but it only runs once the config access point is started, and no 'Access Point started' line appears in the soak."
      ],
      "status": "RESOLVED - config-portal scan timer",
      "why_it_matters": "The radio duty-cycles a ~2.9s scan every ~13s for the whole run, which is real power draw on a battery device, and it is the same scan path that produced the permanent 'No AP found' loop in the hidden-SSID case.",
      "impact_on_verdict": "None: it is not a crash, not a memory leak and not a disconnect. It is recorded as an open observation, not as a FAIL.",
      "caller": "WifiConfigurationAp::scan_timer_, created at managed_components/78__esp-wifi-connect/wifi_configuration_ap.cc:113 and armed with esp_timer_start_once(self->scan_timer_, 10 * 1000000) at wifi_configuration_ap.cc:861",
      "arithmetic": "A one-shot timer re-armed 10 s after each scan finishes, plus about 2.879 s of scan time, gives the measured 12,879 ms cycle. Observed scan starts 867726 -> then StaScanDone 870580 -> next StaScanDone 883459 -> 896339 -> 909219, interval exactly 12879 ms.",
      "why_the_caller_was_not_obvious_at_first": [
        "'rpc_req: Scan start Req' only says the host issued the request; the config-portal component does not print a banner per scan.",
        "The component logs 'Access Point started' exactly once, when it enters config mode, which happened in cp2-wake-02.txt rather than inside the soak.",
        "BluFi and the WifiStation retry path both print their own lines on every scan, and neither appears here, which is what ruled them out."
      ],
      "verdict": "Expected behaviour of the config portal, not a leak and not a defect. It is recorded because the device stays in config mode indefinitely while the saved AP is absent, so it runs a soft AP plus a scan every 12.9 s the whole time. For a mains-powered device this is only a note; for a battery device it is a real power consideration, and it is the same fallback path to review when the hidden-SSID reconnect gap is fixed."
    },
    "context": {
      "state_during_the_whole_soak": "config mode",
      "entered_config_mode_at": "2026-09-22T18:19:55 (captured in cp2-wake-02.txt at 68680 ms uptime, i.e. before the soak started)",
      "evidence": "The soak captures contain no WiFi log lines of their own because nothing changed; the config AP was started once, before the first soak segment, and stayed up for all 31.3 minutes.",
      "consequence": "This is the reason no NETWORK_EVENT appeared: the device had already reached its terminal state for this boot before the soak began."
    }
  },
